+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql cannot be applied to production data
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` makes `users.email` `NOT NULL` and unique
+case-insensitively. It works on a fresh database, but it cannot be applied to
+the production snapshot (`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`) without changing or
+deleting customer rows, which this migration is explicitly not allowed to do.
+
+## Why
+
+The snapshot already violates both constraints:
+
+- **Duplicate emails (case-insensitive):** ids 1, 4 and 23 all map to
+  `ana.silva@example.com`.
+  - id 1: `ana.silva@example.com` (Ana Silva)
+  - id 4: `Ana.Silva@example.com` (Dev Silva)
+  - id 23: `ana.silva@example.com` (Ana Silva)
+- **Missing email:** id 17 (`Goran Okafor`) has `email = NULL`.
+
+A real `NOT NULL` constraint cannot be created while id 17 exists, and a
+`UNIQUE`/unique-index constraint cannot be created while ids 1, 4 and 23 exist.
+SQLite cannot add either constraint in place anyway, so the migration rebuilds
+the table; the copy step fails on the violating rows. Because the rebuild runs
+in a single transaction and only drops the old table after a successful copy,
+a failed run leaves all customer rows unchanged.
+
+There is no schema-only way to satisfy the request without either:
+
+- failing on the existing data (the current, safe state), or
+- altering/deleting the conflicting rows (forbidden: Support owns that data),
+- or silently enforcing the rule only for new rows (triggers/partial indexes),
+  which would leave production violating the invariants while pretending the
+  constraint exists. That would be a workaround, so it was not done.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Support must resolve the data first:
+
+1. Decide which of ids 1, 4 and 23 is the real customer, and merge or reassign
+   the others to distinct email addresses.
+2. Give id 17 a valid email address, or decide how that account should be
+   handled.
+
+Once the snapshot has no case-insensitive duplicates and no NULL emails,
+`002_unique_email.sql` can be applied unchanged: it preserves `id`, `email`
+and `name` exactly for every remaining row.
